From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Adam Matthews <52178922+adamsthws@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 30 Oct 2026 18:50:30 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] emulator: bridge the Wi-Fi peer to a host TAP device
 (--net-tap)

The built-in network (src/netstack.py) is the device's whole world: it is its
router, DHCP server and DNS, and the host reaches it only through 127.0.0.1
port forwards, one port at a time. That cannot put the device on a real LAN
beside other machines, as a plug on a home Wi-Fi is - reached at its own
address by any tool, by every protocol, with the LAN's own router handing
out its address.

--net-tap IFACE swaps that network for src/tapnet.py: every Ethernet frame
the device sends through the Wi-Fi peer goes to the host's TAP device IFACE,
and every frame written there is delivered to the device, on device time as
the built-in stack moves its sockets' bytes. It answers nothing itself; the
network the TAP device is part of does. The device is made by the caller
(ip tuntap add dev IFACE mode tap user $USER), so the emulator itself needs
no privileges. The options only the built-in network has are refused beside
it, not ignored.
---
